.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAG-LIST-LISTENERS 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eag-list-listeners \- list the ports the API gateway answers on
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eag list-listeners
.SH DESCRIPTION
Lists the ports the API gateway answers on: the namespace each serves,
whether it speaks HTTP or HTTPS, and for an HTTPS one the certificate it
terminates TLS with \- down to its subject, issuer, fingerprint and
expiry date.
.PP
This is the one
.B eag
action that reports on the gateway process rather than on the route
table. A listener is configuration
.RI ( euclid.modules.eag.listeners ,
read once at start\-up), so nothing in the database knows what the
gateway is actually bound to, and there is no other way to ask.
.PP
Read\-only, deliberately. A port cannot be moved or switched to HTTPS
through the API: that is a configuration change and a restart, because a
listener that could be re\-bound remotely is a way to take an
installation off the network with one call.
.SH OUTPUT
Each listener is reported as:
.TP
.I namespace
The namespace whose routes this port serves. Empty means it serves every
route, which is what a single\-listener installation gets.
.TP
.I port
The TCP port.
.TP
.I protocol
.I http
or
.IR https .
.TP
.I serving
Whether the ports are actually bound. A listener whose port was taken,
or whose certificate could not be loaded, is still listed \- the module
keeps running so the route table can be managed \- but nothing it names
is being answered. Look in the log for why.
.TP
.I certificate
The certificate an HTTPS listener serves, under the name it is stored in
.BR euclid-cli-ekm (1)
with. This is the name the listener
.I uses
rather than the one it named: a listener that names none takes the
conventional name for its namespace, and an empty field here would send
somebody looking for a certificate that exists.
.I certificateConfigured
is what the configuration actually wrote, so the two cases can be told
apart. Empty for an HTTP listener.
.TP
.I certificateFound
Whether that certificate is stored. False for an HTTPS listener means
the port has never come up: the certificate is generated when a listener
starts, so one that is still missing is one that never got that far.
.TP
.IR certificateSubject ", " certificateIssuer ", " certificateSerialNumber ", " certificateFingerprint ", " certificateSubjectAltNames
Read out of the certificate when it was stored.
.I certificateFingerprint
is what somebody compares against when asked whether to trust a
self-signed one.
.TP
.I certificateGenerated
Whether euclid minted this itself because the listener needed something
to start with, rather than being given one. Callers reject a self-signed
certificate until they are told to trust it, so which of the two this is
decides whether the port works for anybody who has not been told about
it. Replace one with
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-import-certificate (1)
under the same name.
.TP
.IR certificateNotBefore ", " certificateNotAfter ", " certificateExpired
The validity period, and whether it has passed.
.I certificateExpired
is what answers "is this port about to stop working" \- an expired
certificate is only reported, never refused, so the port keeps serving
the callers whose clients do not check while a replacement is fetched.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The call succeeded, including when the gateway has no usable listener at
all. The listeners are printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
No administrator session, or the server rejected the request.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show what the gateway is serving and on what:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eag list-listeners
.fi
.RE
.PP
Find the HTTPS ports still on a certificate euclid generated for itself:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eag list-listeners \e
    | jq '.listeners[] | select(.certificateGenerated == true)'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check what is about to expire:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eag list-listeners \e
    | jq '.listeners[] | {port, certificate, certificateNotAfter}'
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eag (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eag-list-routes (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-list-certificates (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-import-certificate (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-create-certificate (1)
